#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152948} :activity/id #uuid "bb4b44ed-5cd5-537a-880d-adb3d446a517" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153431 :tutorial/content "We can apply quantifiers to predicates with more than one variable.\n\nFor example, the statement\n\n$$\\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n > m$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *For all $m \\in \\mathbb{N},$ there exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $n > m.$*\n\nThis statement is true. Informally, it states that given *any* natural number ${\\color{blue}{m}},$ there is an integer ${\\color{red}{n}}$ that's larger than ${\\color{blue}{m}}.$\n\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{1}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{2}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{2}} > {\\color{blue}{1}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{2}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{3}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{3}} > {\\color{blue}{2}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\in \\mathbb N$ and we select ${\\color{red}{n}}={\\color{red}{4}}\\in \\mathbb Z,$ we have ${\\color{red}{4}} > {\\color{blue}{3}}$ which is true. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nAnd so on. It's easy to see that this statement is true since for any $m\\in\\mathbb N,$ we can simply select $n = m+1,$ and thus $n > m.$\n\n**Watch out!** The order in which the quantifiers are stated is very important. If we swap the quantifiers in this example, we get $$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n > m$$ which reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that for all $m \\in \\mathbb{N},$ $n > m.$*\n\nThis new statement has an entirely different meaning! Informally, it states that there is an integer $n$ that's larger than *every* natural number, which is false!\n\nHowever, quantifiers *of the same type* can be swapped. For example,\n\n$$\\exists m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, n > m \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, \\exists m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, n > m.$$\n\nFinally, if we have two or more quantifiers of the same type defined over the same domain, we can write only one quantifier followed by the corresponding variables.\n\nFor example, the (false) statement $$\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\forall y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y$$ can be written more compactly as $$\\forall \\, x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y.$$\n\nSimilarly, the (true) statement $$\\begin{aligned}∃x\\in R,\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x\\le y\\end{aligned}$$ can be written as $$\\exists \\, x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\leq y.$$\n\nWe'll learn more about determining the truth value of a statement containing multiple quantifiers later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "992d8409-4fd0-588b-8307-afdd3dcaae09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7942 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df03f699-9bf6-5301-9f32-5a1b82728cef" :step/math-academy-id 145948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151817 :question/id #uuid "aca83a15-ecdd-50ad-b384-b27d1f534497" :question/math-academy-id "e-12746"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401797}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "7a975b0a-46f1-5b5b-b4d1-80613a1bc7ac" :step/math-academy-id 145949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153437 :tutorial/content "Consider the following quantified expression\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n\nThis expression has two quantifiers. The **scope** of each quantifier is the range in the expression where the quantifier is applied. We can use parentheses to avoid ambiguity in complex expressions.\n\nIn this example, the scope of the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists y}$ is the following:\n\n$$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists y} \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\overbrace{x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∃y}} \\, \\big),$$\n\nand the scope of $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall x}$ is the following:\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\overbrace{y < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∀x}} \\, \\big).$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7034115f-f0c7-508f-8cdd-fd5c4a8380f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7998 :tutorial/title "The Scope of a Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "ac12610e-248a-5d1f-9e23-576263a1e1c9" :step/math-academy-id 145953 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153449 :tutorial/content "The occurrence of a variable is called **bound** if it is placed right next to a quantifier or lies within the scope of a quantifier that uses the same variable. Otherwise, the occurrence is called **free**.\n\nFor example, let's examine the occurrences of variables in the following statement:\n\n$$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big).$$\n\n- The following occurrence of $y$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists}{:}$ $$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\exists} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{y} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\text{f}}}}{x} \\!\\! > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall}{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\forall} \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{x} \\!\\! \\in \\mathbb{R}, y < 10 \\, \\big)$$\n- The following occurrence of $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $\\exists y$ that uses $y{:}$ $$\\exists y \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\overbrace{x > 0 \\Rightarrow \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\!\\! \\underset{\\substack{\\phantom{.} \\[4pt] \\uparrow \\[6pt] \\large\\phantom{.}\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{\\text{b}}}}{y} \\!\\! < 10}^{\\textrm{scope of ∃y}} \\, \\big)$$\n\nSo, in our expression, we have:\n\n- two occurrences of $y,$ both of which are bound, and\n- two occurrences of $x,$ where one is free, and another is bound.\n\n$$\\exists \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{y} \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big( \\, \\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x} > 0 \\Rightarrow \\big( \\, \\forall \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{y} < 10 \\, \\big) \\, \\big)$$\n\nDistinguishing between free and bound occurrences is important since we are allowed to substitute values into free occurrences of variables. In particular, since the first occurrence of $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x}$ is free, we can substitute concrete values for this variable (but not for the other occurrence of $\\bbox[2pt,Moccasin]{x}$).\n\nTherefore, the above expression can be viewed as a predicate of one variable ($\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{x}$) since all occurrences of $y$ are bound." :tutorial/id #uuid "d98c614c-0abc-540c-a882-40069bc979d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8313 :tutorial/title "Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"} :step/id #uuid "e67bda2d-11a8-533c-9ec8-a842ae2c208f" :step/math-academy-id 145954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151819 :question/id #uuid "eb54c96d-6dd5-5edd-b6a6-460d18645a72" :question/math-academy-id "e-12748"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390218}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"} :step/id #uuid "349da444-94cc-5fbc-9a91-cc3cf599c78d" :step/math-academy-id 145955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153436 :tutorial/content "Consider the statement\n\n$$\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n$$\n\nthat reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m > n.$\n\nIs the statement true or false? To figure this out, we proceed as described below.\n\nFirst, let's write down the inner predicate and determine its truth set: $$P(m): \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n.$$ Notice that this is a predicate in the variable $m$ since $m$ is a free variable in the $P(m)$ statement.\n\nThe predicate $P(m)$ is false for all values $m \\in \\mathbb {N}.$ For example, $$P(1) : \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, 1 > n$$ is false since we can take $n=1.$ Similarly, $$P(2): \\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, 2 > n$$ is false, since we can take $n=2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m$ for any value of $m.$ As a result, the truth set of $P(m)$ is $T_P=\\emptyset.$\n\nNow, notice that we can write our original statement as follows: $$\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n \\: \\equiv \\: \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(m).$$ This statement is true whenever the *inner* predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $\\mathbb N.$ However, this is not the case, as we discovered above.\n\nTherefore, the statement $\\forall\\, m \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, m > n$ is false." :tutorial/id #uuid "860b857c-2cd9-531e-8868-db58f1c6a835" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7997 :tutorial/title "The Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "893b05d5-81e8-5350-beac-4c5747ea81d9" :step/math-academy-id 145958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151818 :question/id #uuid "6434be6e-ad3f-572b-b64d-ead988cc2ca4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12747"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381818}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "913d2921-e79c-586a-9141-9bfd4b5e7097" :step/math-academy-id 145959}] :activity/title "Nested Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382}]}
